2026-03-09 23:40:26 [simon@midnightblue] is branch instruction on 6502 relative to the current instruction or the next one?
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] On the 6502 processor, branch instructions are relative to the next instruction, not the
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] current one.
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] The branch offset is added to the program counter after it has already been incremented to
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] point to the next instruction. This means:
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] - When a branch instruction is fetched, the PC points to the branch opcode
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] - The PC is incremented to point to the branch offset byte
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] - The PC is incremented again to point to the next instruction
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] - The offset is added to this PC value
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] The offset is a signed 8-bit value (-128 to +127), so branches can jump:
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] - Forward up to 127 bytes
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] - Backward up to 128 bytes
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] Example:
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] BEQ $F0  ; Branch if equal to label
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] NOP      ; This is the "next instruction" the offset is relative to
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] This is why you sometimes see `$FE` (or -2) used to branch to the branch instruction itself,
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] creating an infinite loop.
2026-03-09 23:40:38 [deepseek-chat] 
