2026-03-14 17:31:31 [simon@midnightblue] what 6502 instruction enables interrupts?
2026-03-14 17:31:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] `CLI` (Clear Interrupt disable flag)
2026-03-14 17:31:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-03-14 17:31:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] This clears the I flag in the status register, allowing maskable interrupts (IRQ) to be processed.
2026-03-14 17:31:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] The complementary instruction `SEI` sets the I flag to disable interrupts.
2026-03-14 17:31:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-03-14 17:32:02 [simon@midnightblue] vectors for 6520 interrupts vectors
2026-03-14 17:32:04 [claude-haiku-4-5] The 6502 interrupt vectors are:
2026-03-14 17:32:04 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-03-14 17:32:04 [claude-haiku-4-5] - IRQ/BRK: `FFFE-FFFF` (maskable interrupt)
2026-03-14 17:32:04 [claude-haiku-4-5] - NMI: `FFFA-FFFB` (non-maskable interrupt)
2026-03-14 17:32:04 [claude-haiku-4-5] - RESET: `FFFC-FFFD` (reset vector)
2026-03-14 17:32:04 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-03-14 17:32:04 [claude-haiku-4-5] Each vector is 16-bit (2 bytes, little-endian), holding the address where execution jumps when that
2026-03-14 17:32:04 [claude-haiku-4-5] interrupt occurs.
2026-03-14 17:32:04 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-03-14 17:32:58 [simon@midnightblue] pins?
2026-03-14 17:33:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] 6502 interrupt pins:
2026-03-14 17:33:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-03-14 17:33:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] - IRQ (pin 26) - maskable interrupt input (active low)
2026-03-14 17:33:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] - NMI (pin 6) - non-maskable interrupt input (active low)
2026-03-14 17:33:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] - RESET (pin 40) - reset input (active low)
2026-03-14 17:33:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-03-14 17:33:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] All are active-low, typically pulled high with resistors when not asserted.
2026-03-14 17:33:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-03-14 17:33:27 [simon@midnightblue] interrupt vectors and 6502 pins
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] The 6502 microprocessor has 40 pins, including:
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] - Address bus (16 pins: A0-A15)
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] - Data bus (8 pins: D0-D7)
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] - Control pins: RDY, IRQ, NMI, RES, SO, SYNC, R/W, Φ0, Φ1, Φ2
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] - Power: Vss (GND), Vcc (+5V)
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] • Interrupt vectors* (stored at fixed memory locations):
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] - FFFA-FFFB: NMI (Non-Maskable Interrupt)
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] - FFFC-FFFD: RESET
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] - FFFE-FFFF: IRQ/BRK (Maskable Interrupt)
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] NMI is edge-triggered and cannot be disabled. IRQ can be masked using the I flag.
2026-03-14 17:33:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-14 17:34:20 [simon@midnightblue] IRQ and NMI pins numbers?
2026-03-14 17:34:24 [deepseek-chat] On the 6502:
2026-03-14 17:34:24 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-14 17:34:24 [deepseek-chat] - IRQ (Interrupt Request): Pin 4
2026-03-14 17:34:24 [deepseek-chat] - NMI (Non-Maskable Interrupt): Pin 6
2026-03-14 17:34:24 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-14 17:34:24 [deepseek-chat] Both are active-low inputs (asserted when pulled to ground).
2026-03-14 17:34:24 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-14 17:34:40 [simon@midnightblue] and reset?
2026-03-14 17:34:44 [deepseek-chat] • RES* (Reset): Pin 40
2026-03-14 17:34:44 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-14 17:34:44 [deepseek-chat] It's an active-low input. When held low for at least two clock cycles, it triggers a reset sequence
2026-03-14 17:34:44 [deepseek-chat] and jumps to the address at FFFC-FFFD.
2026-03-14 17:34:44 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-14 17:35:19 [simon@midnightblue] can reset be disabled?
2026-03-14 17:35:23 [deepseek-chat] No. RESET cannot be disabled on the 6502. It's a hardware-level input that always triggers
2026-03-14 17:35:23 [deepseek-chat] a full processor reset when asserted (held low). There's no software control or status flag to mask
2026-03-14 17:35:23 [deepseek-chat] or ignore it.
2026-03-14 17:35:23 [deepseek-chat] 
