2026-03-26 19:16:34 [simon@midnightblue] pricing for vertex ai search
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] Vertex AI Search pricing is primarily based on the number of queries and the type of data indexed.
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] Here is a summary of the current cost structure:
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Search Queries: You are charged per 1,000 queries. The price varies depending on the
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] edition (Standard vs. Enterprise).
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Indexing/Data Storage: You are charged for the amount of data stored and processed for
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] indexing (per GB per month).
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Enterprise Features: Advanced features like Generative AI (Search and Summarization)
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] often carry additional costs per query.
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Recommendations:*
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Google Cloud Pricing Calculator: Use the [official
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] calculator](https://cloud.google.com/products/calculator) to input your expected monthly query
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] volume for an accurate estimate.
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Free Tier: Vertex AI Search offers a free tier for specific features; check the [Vertex
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] AI Search pricing page](https://cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/pricing) for the most
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] current thresholds.
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you are looking to implement this in your app, I can provide a template for calling the Vertex
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] AI Search API once you have your project configured. Would you like to see that?
2026-03-26 19:16:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:17:40 [simon@midnightblue] is there a deno sdk or can you provide simple example so i can see what i am getting into
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] Vertex AI Search (part of Google Cloud's Agent Builder) does not have a dedicated Deno SDK.
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] However, because it exposes a standard REST API, you can interact with it using Deno's native
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] `fetch` API.
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] Regarding pricing, Vertex AI Search is billed based on "Search Queries" (the number of requests)
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] and the volume of data indexed. You can find the detailed breakdown on the [Google Cloud Vertex AI
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] Search pricing page](https://cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/pricing).
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] Here is a simple example of how to query a Vertex AI Search engine using Deno:
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] async function searchVertex(query, projectId, location, dataStoreId) {
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	const url = `https://discoveryengine.googleapis.com/v1beta/projects/${projectId}/locations/${location}/collections/default_collection/dataStores/${dataStoreId}/servingConfigs/default_search:search`;
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	const response = await fetch(url, {
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		method: 'POST',
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		headers: {
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			'Authorization': `Bearer ${Deno.env.get('GOOGLE_ACCESS_TOKEN')}`,
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			'Content-Type': 'application/json',
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		},
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		body: JSON.stringify({
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			query: query,
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			pageSize: 5
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		}),
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	});
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	if (!response.ok) {
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		throw new Error(`Error: ${response.statusText}`);
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	}
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	return await response.json();
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Usage
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] const results = await searchVertex('your-query', 'your-project-id', 'global', 'your-datastore-id');
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] console.log(results);
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] Key Considerations:
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Authentication: You will need to generate an OAuth 2.0 access token (using Google Cloud
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] Service Account credentials) to include in the `Authorization` header.
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Environment: Ensure you have the `GOOGLEAPPLICATIONCREDENTIALS` environment
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] variable set or use a library like `google-auth-library-js` (via npm specifiers in Deno) to handle
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] token refreshing.
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Setup: You must create an "App" in the [Google Cloud Agent Builder
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] console](https://console.cloud.google.com/gen-app-builder) before you can call the API.
2026-03-26 19:17:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
