2026-03-28 23:41:03 [simon@midnightblue] can i use a temp variable instead of an index for my display line loop
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] You only have X and Y scratch registers in PIO. There are no other variables or RAM
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] access.
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] For 288 lines (which exceeds the `set` limit of 31), your options are:
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] • 1. Nested loops* (uses both registers):
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] set x, 8      ; 9 iterations (0-8)
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] .outer:
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 	set y, 31   ; 32 iterations
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] .inner:
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 	; ... line data ...
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 	jmp y--, .inner
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 	jmp x--, .outer
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] • 2. Load from FIFO* (uses one register):
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] pull          ; main CPU sends 288 into FIFO
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] out x, 32     ; X = 288
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] .loop:
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 	; ... line data ...
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 	jmp x--, .loop
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] • 3. Preserve register* (temporarily stash X/Y):
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] mov osr, x    ; save X to OSR
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] ; ... use X for something else ...
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] mov x, osr    ; restore X
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] If you need X for pixel counting inside the line, use Y for the line counter (or vice
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] versa). You cannot declare a separate temporary variable.
2026-03-28 23:41:50 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:37:13 [simon@midnightblue] deno upgrade
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] Clever approach using PIO pin direction toggling to generate composite video levels with a passive
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] resistor DAC.
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] • Notable technique:* Using `in x,25` / `mov x,isr` to load large constants (>31) into
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] registers. This shifts immediate values into the ISR then moves them to X, avoiding the 5-bit `set`
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] limitation. This creates the cycle-count delays needed for PAL timing (64µs lines at 150MHz).
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] • Circuit logic:*
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] - Drive low = sync (0V)
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] - Input/high-Z = black (biased by trimpot divider)
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] - Drive high = white (via 330Ω/75Ω divider)
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] • Issue:* `voltageTest()` is defined after main's return and unreachable. If you want to
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] calibrate levels, move it before main or add a forward declaration and call it from main.
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] • PIO observation:* The `vidblit` program runs autonomously without CPU intervention (no TX
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] FIFO pushes), generating a fixed stripe pattern. If you want to output video data from RAM, you'll
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] need to use `piosmput()` or autopull with a DMA channel feeding the FIFO.
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] Ensure your `CMakeLists.txt` includes:
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] pico_generate_pio_header(vidbit ${CMAKE_CURRENT_LIST_DIR}/vidblit.pio)
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:41:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:43:09 [kimi-k2.5] Build config looks correct. The `picogeneratepio_header` matches the PIO file location.
2026-03-29 04:43:09 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:43:09 [kimi-k2.5] Minor notes:
2026-03-29 04:43:09 [kimi-k2.5] - `OUTPUTNAME "vidbit.elf"` is redundant (the `picoaddextraoutputs` already
2026-03-29 04:43:09 [kimi-k2.5] generates `.elf`, `.uf2`, `.bin` from the base target name)
2026-03-29 04:43:09 [kimi-k2.5] - Disabling USB reset interfaces is wise for video output to avoid frame drops during enumeration
2026-03-29 04:43:09 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:43:09 [kimi-k2.5] The RP2350 HSTX peripheral might interest you if you want to move beyond bit-banged composite to
2026-03-29 04:43:09 [kimi-k2.5] HDMI/DVI output later, though your resistor DAC approach is elegant for PAL/NTSC.
2026-03-29 04:43:09 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:44:29 [simon@midnightblue] for digital tv to recognise sync i may need to modify vsync from single period
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] Digital TVs often require the standard vsync pulse sequence (pre-equalizing, broad pulses,
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] post-equalizing) rather than a simple long blanking period. They detect vsync by looking for the
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] 27.3µs broad pulses at half-line frequency, not just a missing signal.
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] • PAL vsync structure (lines):*
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] 1. 5 lines: Pre-equalizing (2.35µs low / 29.65µs high, twice per line)
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] 2. 5 lines: Vertical sync (27.3µs low / 4.7µs high, twice per line)
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] 3. 5 lines: Post-equalizing (same as pre-equalizing)
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] • Cycle counts at 150MHz:*
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] - 2.35µs = 353 cycles
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] - 4.7µs = 705 cycles
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] - 27.3µs = 4095 cycles
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] - 29.65µs = 4448 cycles
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] - Half-line = 4800 cycles
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] • PIO approach:*
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] Replace your `vblank` section with nested loops using the `in` trick to load large constants.
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] Example for broad pulses (simplified):
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:47:40 [kimi-k2.5] ; Broad pulse line (27.3µs low, 4.7µs high, repeated)
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] set y, 4					; 5 lines
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] broad_line:
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	; First half: 4095 cycles low
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	set x, 127
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	in x, 5					; 127 << 5 = 3968
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	mov x, isr
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	set pins, 0				; sync low
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] broad_low_1:
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	jmp x--, broad_low_1	; 3968 cycles
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	nop [126]				; +127 = 4095
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	set pins, 1				; high (black)
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	set x, 22
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	in x, 5					; 22 << 5 = 704
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	mov x, isr
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] broad_high_1:
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	jmp x--, broad_high_1	; 704 cycles
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	nop [0]					; +1 = 705
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	; Second half (repeat low/high sequence for 64µs total)
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	set x, 127
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	in x, 5
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	mov x, isr
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	set pins, 0
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] broad_low_2:
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	jmp x--, broad_low_2
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	nop [126]
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	set pins, 1
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	set x, 22
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	in x, 5
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	mov x, isr
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] broad_high_2:
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	jmp x--, broad_high_2
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	nop [0]
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 	jmp y--, broad_line
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] If instruction space is tight (you have ~12 instructions left in your current 32-instruction
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] program), you can often get away with just the 5 broad pulse lines and skip pre/post
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] equalizing—many digital TVs will lock with just the broad pulse train. Generate 5 lines of (4095
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] cycles low / 705 cycles high) repeated twice per line.
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] Alternatively, use the CPU to trigger a DMA transfer of pre-calculated sync patterns into the PIO
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] FIFO during blanking, keeping the PIO program simple.
2026-03-29 04:47:41 [kimi-k2.5] 
