2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] Regarding the issues you are seeing, there are two separate causes at play:
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Wobbly Sync (Register Collision)*
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your `vidblit.pio` file, the `X` register is being used for two different loops at the same
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] time. You set `X` to the total line count before the `line:` label, but inside the `active:` block,
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] you use `set x, 9` for the `stripe:` loop. When the stripe loop finishes, `X` becomes `-1`, which
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] causes your outer `jmp x--, line` to terminate after a single line. This breaks the PAL frame
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] timing. You will need to stash the line counter in the `OSR` or `ISR` registers during the inner
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] loops to preserve it.
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Grey Stripes (Voltage Levels)*
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] When the PIO sets the pin to an input (`set pindirs, 0`), the pin floats and relies entirely on
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] your 3.3kΩ trimpot to pull the voltage up against the TV's 75Ω termination to ground.
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Adjusting the trimpot should help tune this floating "black" level to the standard ~0.3V.
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • For the "white" level (`set pindirs, 1`), driving 3.3V through a 330Ω resistor into a 75Ω load
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] yields a maximum of roughly ~0.61V. Standard PAL white is 1.0V, which is why your whites are
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] appearing as a slightly dim grey. Lowering the 330Ω resistor slightly would brighten the white
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] level.
2026-03-29 09:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:07:45 [simon@midnightblue] oh oops, on the using x in outer and inner, is there other place i can stash the counter?
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] That is excellent news getting an image on the TV! The wobbles and grey stripes are definitely
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] symptoms of the timing getting thrown off by that clobbered `X` register.
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since you only have `X` and `Y` for standard scratch registers, the standard trick in PIO is to
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] stash variables in the `OSR` (Output Shift Register) or `ISR` (Input Shift Register) when you
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] aren't actively shifting data through them.
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is how you can stash the outer loop counter in the `OSR`:
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] line:
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	mov	osr, x		; Stash outer loop counter
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; active = 52 µs = 7,800 cycles = 25 × 312
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pins, 1
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 0
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	y, 24
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] active:
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	x, 9		; Inner loop uses X safely now
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] stripe:
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	nop	[10]
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 1
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	nop	[10]
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 0
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	nop	[6]
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	x--, stripe
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--, active
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; hblank = 12 µs = 1,800 cycles = 25 x 72
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pins, 0
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 1
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	y, 24
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] hblank:
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	nop	[31]
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	nop	[31]
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	nop	[6]
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--, hblank
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	mov	x, osr		; Restore outer loop counter
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	x--, line
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] For the C++ configuration, you need to tell the state machine which pin the `set` instructions
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] should affect. You can add `smconfigsetsetpins` right after you get the default
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] config:
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	config = vidblit_program_get_default_config(offset1);
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	sm_config_set_set_pins(&config, VID_PIN, 1);
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int pioStatus = pio_sm_init(pio, VidStateMachine, offset1, &config);
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] Give those a try and see if it stabilizes your sync and cleans up your luma! Let me know if you
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] need to adjust the timing further.
2026-03-29 09:08:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:15:13 [simon@midnightblue] new topic - factors of 7800
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Excellent progress getting an image on the TV! The wobbly sync and noise are almost certainly
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] caused by that loop counter collision throwing off your timing.
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Stashing the PIO Counter*
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since the PIO only has `x` and `y` for scratch registers, you can safely stash one of them in the
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Output Shift Register (`osr`) or Input Shift Register (`isr`), provided you aren't currently using
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] them to shift data.
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Before you set `x` for your inner `stripe` loop, save it using `mov osr, x`. Once the inner loop
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] completes, restore it using `mov x, osr` just before your outer `jmp x--, line`.
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Factors of 7800*
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] The prime factorization of 7800 is 2³ × 3 × 5² × 13.
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the complete list of its factors, which should be helpful for calculating new PIO delay
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] loops:
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 13, 15, 20, 24, 25, 26, 30, 39, 40, 50, 52, 60, 65, 75, 78, 100, 104,
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 120, 130, 150, 156, 195, 200, 260, 300, 312, 325, 390, 520, 600, 650, 780, 975, 1300, 1560, 1950,
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2600, 3900, 7800.
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you need to keep loop counters under 32 (to fit in a standard `set` instruction without needing
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] to shift bits), the factor pairs near that limit are:
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 24 × 325
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 25 × 312
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 26 × 300
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 30 × 260
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know if you would like me to write out the updated PIO code block with the `osr` stash
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] implemented or the `VID_PIN` config applied.
2026-03-29 09:15:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:18:59 [simon@midnightblue] in pio what does this set y to
