2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Congratulations on getting an image on the TV! That is a massive milestone for a custom video
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] signal generator.
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the answers to your questions:
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Stashing a counter in PIO*
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you need a third register and are not actively shifting data in or out at that exact moment, you
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] can stash `x` or `y` in the `osr` (Output Shift Register) or `isr` (Input Shift Register).
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • To save: `mov osr, x`
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • To restore: `mov x, osr`
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Factors of 7800*
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] The prime factorization of 7800 is 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 × 5 × 5 × 13.
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you are looking for loop counter pairs (especially where one number fits in a 5-bit `set`
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] instruction, which maxes out at 31), here are some useful combinations:
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 20 × 390
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 24 × 325
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 25 × 312
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 26 × 300
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 30 × 260
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. What does that PIO snippet set `y` to?*
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] It sets `y` to 400.
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the breakdown of why:
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `set y, 25`: Sets `y` to `25` (binary `11001`).
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `in y, 28`: Takes the lowest 28 bits of `y` and shifts them into the `isr`. Assuming the PIO is
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] set to its default right-shift behavior, data enters at the Most Significant Bit (MSB) and
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] pushes existing data down. Because it shifts in 28 bits into a 32-bit register, those bits end up
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] shifted left by 4 positions (32 - 28 = 4).
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Mathematically, this is `25 << 4`, which equals `400`.
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `mov y, isr`: Copies that `400` back into `y`.
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (Note: If your PIO was configured for left-shift, it would just equal 25, as the data would enter
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] at the LSB).*
2026-03-29 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
