2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Configuring VID_PIN in the PIO config*
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] To configure the pins for `set` and `out` instructions in your C++ setup, you will need to add
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] these to your configuration before initializing the state machine:
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] sm_config_set_set_pins(&config, VID_PIN, 1);
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] sm_config_set_out_pins(&config, VID_PIN, 1);
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Stashing the outer loop counter*
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since you are reusing `x` and breaking your outer loop, you can safely stash your outer loop
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] counter in the `osr` (Output Shift Register), provided you aren't using `out` instructions to read
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] from it.
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Save it: `mov osr, x`
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Restore it: `mov x, osr`
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. Factors of 7800*
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] The prime factorization of 7800 is 2³ × 3 × 5² × 13.
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the most useful integer pairs for loop counting:
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 25 × 312
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 30 × 260
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 39 × 200
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 50 × 156
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 52 × 150
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 60 × 130
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 75 × 104
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 78 × 100
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 4. PIO `in` instruction behavior*
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Regarding `set y, 25`, `in y, 28`, and `mov y, isr`: the final value of `y` depends on your `in`
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] shift direction (which defaults to right). Assuming the ISR starts empty:
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • If shifting right (default): The 28 least significant bits of `y` (which is 25) are
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] pushed into the ISR from the top (MSB). This leaves 4 empty bits at the bottom, effectively
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] multiplying 25 by 2⁴ (16). The result is 400.
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • If shifting left: The value 25 is pushed into the ISR from the bottom (LSB). The result
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] remains 25.
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 5. Halting the Bash script on error*
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] The cleanest way to prevent the script from copying the file if the build fails is to add `set -e`
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] at the very top of your script. This tells Bash to exit immediately if any command returns a
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] non-zero (error) status.
2026-03-29 09:22:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
