2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PIO Large Value Loading: The sequence `set x, 3`, `in x, 25`, `mov x, isr` relies on the
2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] ISR shift behavior to build large loop counters. By default, the PIO `IN` instruction shifts right.
2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] To build a large number by shifting left, the state machine's `IN` shift direction must be
2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] explicitly configured to 'left' in your C++ code. Additionally, the standard pattern for this is
2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] usually to move the base value into the ISR first, and then shift in `null` padding (e.g., `mov
2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] isr, x` followed by `in null, 25`).
2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PIO Loop Flow: In your `active` block, you have a `jmp x--, active` immediately after the
2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] `stripe` loop, and another one after `hblank`. The first jump will cause the state machine to skip
2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] `hblank` entirely until `x` reaches 0. Once it finally falls through to `hblank`, the second jump
2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] will evaluate with `x` underflowing to `0xFFFFFFFF`, resulting in an unintended massive loop.
2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] • C++ Clock Configuration: The PIO relies on an exact 150MHz clock for your cycle counts.
2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] While the RP2350 defaults to 150MHz, it is generally safer to explicitly configure the system clock
2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] (or the PIO clock divider) in your initialization to guarantee the timing remains accurate.
2026-03-29 09:28:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Build Script Typo: In your bash script, the copy command references `vidbit.elf.uf2`
