2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] Based on your clever resistor network, here is how the standard PAL timings break down for a single
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] line:
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Front Porch (Black / High-Z): ~1.65 µs
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro]    * This is a short black border before the sync pulse.
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro]    * At 150 MHz: ~248 cycles
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Horizontal Sync (Drive 0V): ~4.7 µs
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro]    * This tells the TV to move the beam back to the left.
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro]    * At 150 MHz: 705 cycles
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Back Porch (Black / High-Z): ~5.65 µs
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro]    * This is a longer black border after the sync pulse (which normally contains the color burst,
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] though not needed for monochrome).
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro]    * At 150 MHz: ~847 cycles
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. Active Video (Drive 3.3V / High-Z for pixels): ~52 µs
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro]    * This is your visible drawing area.
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro]    * At 150 MHz: 7,800 cycles
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] So the sequence in your loop would ideally be: Active -> Front Porch -> Sync -> Back Porch
2026-03-29 10:28:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] (or starting from Sync, it doesn't matter as long as it loops).
