2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your horizontal timing math is remarkably accurate. At 150 MHz, 9,600 cycles per 64 µs line is
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfectly on target, and your split of 1,800 cycles for H-blank and 7,800 cycles for active video
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] aligns exactly with the PAL standard. Factoring in the PIO loop overheads, your cycle counting is
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] within a handful of cycles of perfection.
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since the math is solid but the TV is reporting "no signal," the issue is likely rooted in the
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] overall signal structure rather than the horizontal timing. Here are the primary areas to
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] investigate:
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Vertical Sync Structure: This is the most common cause of "no signal" on modern TVs. Your
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] code appears to hold a single state for the entire 72,000-cycle V-blank period. PAL TVs expect
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] continuous horizontal synchronization even during the vertical blanking interval. A proper V-sync
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] requires specific "broad pulses" (sync pulled low for ~27.3 µs, twice per line) so the TV's
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] horizontal PLL doesn't lose lock. Without these serrations, the TV drops the signal.
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Total Line Count: Your outer loops dictate the total lines per frame. It looks like you
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] have 289 active lines (since `isr` is 288 and `jmp y--` runs to -1) plus 25 blank lines, totaling
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 314 lines. Progressive PAL (often called 288p) strictly expects exactly 312 lines. Many modern
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] digital scalers will reject a 314-line signal.
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Voltage Levels (Pin Directions): I notice you are toggling `pindirs` rather than `pins`
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] for the sync pulses, which implies you are using an open-drain setup with external pull-up
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] resistors. Ensure your resistor network correctly produces the standard 1Vpp composite signal,
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] specifically ensuring the sync tip dips exactly ~0.3V below the black level.
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • ISR Shift Logic: Double-check that `set x, 9` followed by `in x, 27` evaluates to exactly
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 288 in the ISR. Depending on your PIO shift direction configuration (left vs. right), the resulting
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] integer might be drastically different than intended, which would silently throw off your
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] macroscopic timings.
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Given the 32-instruction PIO limit, generating proper V-sync broad pulses directly in PIO alongside
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] the active video is notoriously difficult. Many developers handle the V-sync pulse generation using
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] the RP2350's DMA to feed specific sync patterns to the PIO during the vertical blanking lines,
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] freeing up the PIO program to just act as a dumb shift register.
2026-03-29 11:53:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
