2026-03-29 13:46:44 [simon@midnightblue] current testing after TV was happy to sync is no go, can you confirm use of osr to hold a constant is valid
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, using the OSR to hold a constant is a perfectly valid and common technique in PIO.
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, there is a lifecycle issue in the current PIO implementation that will cause the OSR to be
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] overwritten after the first frame.
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because there is no `.wrap_target` directive specified, the program implicitly wraps back to the
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] very first instruction (`pull noblock`) at the end of every video frame.
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. On the first pass, `pull noblock` successfully pulls the `287` you placed in the FIFO into the
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] OSR.
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. At the end of the frame, the `active` loop has decremented the `X` scratch register down to `-1`
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] (`0xFFFFFFFF`).
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. The program wraps back to `pull noblock`. Since the C++ code does not feed the FIFO continually,
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] the FIFO is now empty.
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. By design, when `pull noblock` encounters an empty FIFO, it copies the contents of the `X`
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] register into the OSR.
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 5. Your OSR constant is overwritten with `-1`, which breaks the `vsync` and `active` loop timings
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] for all subsequent frames.
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] To resolve this, you can place a `.wrap_target` directive immediately after the `pull noblock`
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] instruction. This ensures the pull only executes once at state machine startup, allowing the OSR to
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] safely retain your constant indefinitely.
2026-03-29 13:47:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:48:20 [simon@midnightblue] awesome that fixed it!
2026-03-29 13:48:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] That is excellent news!
2026-03-29 13:48:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:48:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] To confirm your observation: yes, using the Output Shift Register (OSR) to hold a constant is a
2026-03-29 13:48:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfectly valid and highly efficient PIO technique. Because your program never executes an `out`
2026-03-29 13:48:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] instruction or a subsequent `pull`, the OSR retains that initial value indefinitely.
2026-03-29 13:48:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:48:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is a great pattern for video generation because it frees up both the X and Y scratch registers
2026-03-29 13:48:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] to act as nested loop counters (like your line and pixel counters) while still giving you access to
2026-03-29 13:48:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] a persistent constant. Pre-loading the FIFO with 287 before enabling the state machine is exactly
2026-03-29 13:48:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] the right way to set this up.
2026-03-29 13:48:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:48:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Please feel free to reach out if you run into any other timing or synchronization quirks with the
2026-03-29 13:48:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] RP2350!
