2026-03-29 13:49:25 [simon@midnightblue] it now looks like my hblank is a bit off, tv is display rough dynamic pattern, not clean stripes
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] The issue is likely caused by the program wrapping back to `pull noblock` at the end of each frame.
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the C++ code only pushes a single value (287) to the FIFO, subsequent `pull noblock`
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] executions will find an empty FIFO. By design in the PIO architecture, if `pull noblock` executes
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] on an empty FIFO, it copies the `X` scratch register into the `OSR`.
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since `X` just finished decrementing to `-1` to exit your active line loop, `OSR` gets overwritten
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] with `-1` (0xFFFFFFFF). This makes all your `y--` and `x--` loops run millions of times on the
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] second frame, destroying the timing.
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] To resolve this, you can place the `.wrap_target` directive immediately after your `pull noblock`
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] instruction. This ensures the PIO only pulls the configuration value once during initialization and
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] safely loops the video generation logic.
2026-03-29 13:49:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your active line and horizontal blanking math is actually perfect—it totals exactly 9,600 cycles
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] (64 µs).
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] The rough, dynamic pattern is likely being caused by the vertical blanking (`vblank`) section.
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Currently, the code holds the signal at the sync level (LOW) for the entire vertical blanking
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] period.
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Analog TVs rely on a continuous rhythm of horizontal sync pulses to keep their horizontal
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] phase-locked loop (PLL) stable. Because the horizontal pulses disappear entirely during your vblank
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] routine, the TV loses horizontal lock. When your active lines begin, the TV is out of sync and
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] takes time to recover, causing the tearing or dynamic pattern.
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] To keep the TV happy, the vertical blanking period needs to maintain that strict 9,600-cycle
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] horizontal rhythm. A common approach for a simplified progressive signal (288p) is:
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Vertical Sync: Output ~3 lines where the signal is mostly LOW, but pulses HIGH briefly
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] every 9,600 cycles.
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Post-VSync Blanking: Output ~21 lines that have normal HSYNC and porches, but the active
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] video portion remains black (LOW).
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Also, as a quick heads-up on PIO timing: `nop [10]` consumes 11 cycles, not 10. Combined with the
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] `jmp`, your current inner vblank loop takes 12 cycles per iteration.
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know if you'd like me to draft the PIO assembly to structure the vertical sync and blanking
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] lines!
2026-03-29 13:50:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:57:44 [simon@midnightblue] simple way to have bootsel held down cause reset using wdt
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the BOOTSEL button shares the flash memory Chip Select (CS) pin, reading it requires
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] briefly disabling interrupts to safely check the pin state without crashing the executing code.
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are reusable functions to read the button and trigger a Watchdog Timer (WDT) reset.
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] #include "hardware/sync.h"
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] #include "hardware/structs/ioqspi.h"
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] #include "hardware/structs/sio.h"
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] #include "hardware/watchdog.h"
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] #include "pico/bootrom.h"
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] bool is_bootsel_pressed() {
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	const uint CS_PIN_INDEX = 1;
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint32_t flags = save_and_disable_interrupts();
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Force CS pin to act as an input
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	hw_write_masked(&ioqspi_hw->io[CS_PIN_INDEX].ctrl,
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 					GPIO_OVERRIDE_LOW << IO_QSPI_GPIO_QSPI_SS_CTRL_OEOVER_LSB,
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 					IO_QSPI_GPIO_QSPI_SS_CTRL_OEOVER_BITS);
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Allow capacitance to discharge
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for (volatile int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i);
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Read the pin state (active low)
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	bool pressed = !(sio_hw->gpio_hi_in & (1u << CS_PIN_INDEX));
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Restore normal CS pin function
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	hw_write_masked(&ioqspi_hw->io[CS_PIN_INDEX].ctrl,
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 					GPIO_OVERRIDE_NORMAL << IO_QSPI_GPIO_QSPI_SS_CTRL_OEOVER_LSB,
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 					IO_QSPI_GPIO_QSPI_SS_CTRL_OEOVER_BITS);
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	restore_interrupts(flags);
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	return pressed;
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] void check_bootsel_and_reset() {
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	if (is_bootsel_pressed()) {
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// For a normal program restart:
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		watchdog_reboot(0, 0, 0);
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// Alternatively, to reboot into the USB bootloader (mass storage):
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// reset_usb_boot(0, 0);
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		while (true); // Halt and wait for the WDT to fire
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] You can drop `checkbootseland_reset()` anywhere inside your main `while (true)` loop.
2026-03-29 13:58:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
