2026-03-29 14:16:55 [simon@midnightblue] typical reset button added to a pico
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the answers to your questions regarding resetting and the BOOTSEL button:
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Where is `resetusbboot` declared?*
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] It is declared in `pico/bootrom.h`.
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Where is `ReadBootSel` declared?*
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] `ReadBootSel` is not a standard Pico SDK function. You are likely thinking of a custom community
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] function. The standard SDK method to read the BOOTSEL button is
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] `picoboardisbootsel_pressed()`, which requires `#include "pico/stdlib.h"`. Reading this
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] button is complex because it shares the QSPI Chip Select pin with the flash memory, so the
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] execution must temporarily pause flash access and run from RAM.
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. `resetusbboot(0, 0)` does not stop the PIO*
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] When jumping to the bootrom, the CPU halts your program, but peripherals like the PIO may continue
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] running or holding their pin states. To ensure the PIO stops producing a video signal, you should
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] explicitly disable it and reset the peripheral block before rebooting.
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] #include "hardware/resets.h"
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] #include "pico/bootrom.h"
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] void rebootToUsb() {
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // Disable state machine and reset the PIO0 hardware block
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro]     pio_sm_set_enabled(pio0, VidStateMachine, false);
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro]     reset_block(RESETS_RESET_PIO0_BITS);
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro]     reset_usb_boot(0, 0);
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 4. Simple way to have BOOTSEL held down cause reset using WDT*
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] You can poll the BOOTSEL button in your main loop and trigger a hardware watchdog reset. This will
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] restart your program normally (unlike `resetusbboot`, which mounts the Pico as a drive).
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] #include "hardware/watchdog.h"
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] void checkBootselAndReset() {
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro]     if (picoboard_is_bootsel_pressed()) {
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro]         pio_sm_set_enabled(pio0, VidStateMachine, false);
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro]         reset_block(RESETS_RESET_PIO0_BITS);
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro]         // Trigger immediate watchdog reset
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro]         watchdog_reboot(0, 0, 0);
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro]     }
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 5. Typical reset button added to a Pico*
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] To add a physical hardware reset button, wire a momentary push-button switch between the
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] RUN pin (Physical Pin 30) and any GND pin (e.g., Physical Pin 28). Pressing it
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulls the RUN pin low, instantly hardware-resetting the RP2350.
2026-03-29 14:17:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:23:39 [simon@midnightblue] frame is looking good, i have a new test of turning tv off and on, which shows I still have somre room for improvement
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] Thank you for sharing the updated files. It is great to hear the frame is rendering well.
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] Regarding the TV struggling to lock on when power cycled, the issue likely stems from the `vblank`
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] implementation in your PIO code.
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] Currently, the `vblank` loop sets the pin to High-Z (black level) and holds it there for 25 entire
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] lines (72,000 cycles). Standard PAL requires uninterrupted synchronization pulses during the
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] vertical blanking interval (specifically, a sequence of pre-equalizing pulses, broad vertical sync
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulses, and post-equalizing pulses).
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the TV receives no sync pulses for 25 lines, its phase-locked loop (PLL) drifts. When the
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] TV is turned on, it takes extra time to find the sync timing once the active lines resume.
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] To improve the signal lock, you will need to maintain horizontal sync pulses throughout the
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] blanking lines, or implement the proper PAL broad sync pulses.
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know if you would like to explore modifying the PIO program to include these continuous sync
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulses.
2026-03-29 22:24:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
