2026-03-29 22:26:53 [simon@midnightblue] can you explain how the color burst signal works and general order of hsync pporces and active line
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the general sequence of a single horizontal video line and how the color burst fits into
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] it.
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Horizontal Line Sequence*
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] A standard horizontal scan line operates in a continuous cycle, typically measured starting from
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] the sync pulse:
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Horizontal Sync (H-Sync): A pulse driven below the black level (sync tip) that triggers
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] the display to return the electron beam (or digital equivalent) to the left side of the screen.
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Back Porch: A short period held at the black blanking level immediately following the
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] H-Sync. This allows the display hardware time to settle after the horizontal retrace.
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Active Video: The visible portion of the line where the luminance (brightness) and
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] chrominance (color) signals are drawn.
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. Front Porch: A brief blanking period at the black level after the active video ends. It
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] provides a buffer before the next H-Sync pulse drops, ensuring the video signal is completely off.
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Color Burst Signal*
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] The color burst is a synchronization reference placed directly on the Back Porch.
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] • What it is: A short, unmodulated burst of the color subcarrier sine wave. For PAL, this
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] is exactly 10 cycles at 4.43361875 MHz.
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Purpose: Color in composite video is encoded using phase and amplitude modulation. To
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] decode the exact hues, the TV needs a precise reference point. The burst synchronizes the TV's
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] internal color oscillator to the exact frequency and phase of the broadcast signal.
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PAL Specifics: PAL stands for "Phase Alternating Line." To correct for transmission phase
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] errors (which cause color tint shifts in NTSC), the phase of the PAL color burst swings between
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] +135° and -135° on alternating lines. The TV averages consecutive lines to cancel out any color
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] distortion.
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] Generating a precise 4.43 MHz color burst via PIO requires very careful clock division, as the
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] subcarrier frequency must be highly accurate for the TV to lock onto the color.
2026-03-29 22:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:30:09 [simon@midnightblue] what other components of rp2350 could help with generating such a signal??
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] It is excellent to hear the framing is taking shape. Interlacing and refining the vertical blanking
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] intervals will certainly improve compatibility across different displays.
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a breakdown of your questions regarding the PAL video signal and the RP2350:
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. The Color Burst Signal
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] The color burst is a short, precise high-frequency sine wave (4.43361875 MHz for PAL) transmitted
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] during the back porch of every horizontal line.
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Purpose: It serves as a frequency and phase reference clock for the television's color
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] decoder. The TV uses this to decode the color (chrominance) information superimposed on the active
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] video signal.
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PAL Specifics: In PAL (Phase Alternating Line), the phase of the color burst alternates
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] by 90 degrees (+135° and -135°) on each consecutive scanline. This allows the TV to automatically
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] cancel out color phase errors (hue shifts) during transmission.
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. General Order of a Horizontal Line
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] A single horizontal scanline progresses in the following order:
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  Front Porch: A brief period at the blanking voltage level (~0.3V). It provides a buffer
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] between the active video and the sync pulse.
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  Horizontal Sync (HSync): A pulse driven to the lowest voltage level (0V). This tells
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] the electron gun (or digital scaler) to return to the left side of the screen.
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3.  Back Porch: A return to the blanking voltage level (~0.3V). The color burst is
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] transmitted here.
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4.  Active Video: The actual picture data, varying between black (~0.3V) and peak white
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] (~1.0V).
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. RP2350 Components for Video Generation
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Beyond the PIO you are currently utilizing, the RP2350 has several hardware features highly suited
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] for video generation:
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • HSTX (High-Speed Transmission): This is a new peripheral specific to the RP2350. It is
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] designed specifically for continuous, high-bandwidth data streams like DVI, HDMI, or high-speed
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] DACs. It handles data shifting and clocking automatically, potentially freeing up PIO state
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] machines.
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • DMA (Direct Memory Access): Essential for moving pixel data from RAM to the PIO (or HSTX)
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] buffers automatically. This allows the CPU to focus entirely on game logic or rendering the next
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] frame without managing the strict timing of the active video line.
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • SIO Interpolators: These can be very useful for fast texture mapping, scaling, or
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] unpacking compressed pixel formats on the fly before sending them to the DMA/PIO.
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Please let me know if you would like to look at implementing DMA or adjusting the PIO timing for
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] interlacing.
2026-03-29 22:30:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
