2026-03-30 19:26:23 [simon@midnightblue] i now want to add a ps2 mouse keyboard pio reader, no code just discussion
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] Adding a PS/2 mouse/keyboard reader via PIO is a good approach. Here are key considerations:
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] • PS/2 Protocol Basics:*
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] - Bidirectional serial protocol at 10-16.7 kHz
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] - Data on single pin with clock on separate pin
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] - 11-bit frames: start(0), 8 data bits, parity, stop(1)
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] - Open-collector signaling requires pull-ups
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] • PIO Design Choices:*
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 1. Pin Configuration:
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - Need 2 pins per device (data, clock)
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - Typically GPIO pull-ups enabled
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - Consider interrupt handling for clock edges
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 2. State Machine Approach:
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - Could use one SM per PS/2 device
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - Clock pin as SET/input for synchronization
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - Data pin sampled on clock falling edges
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 3. Timing Constraints:
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - PS/2 clock ~10-16.7 µs period
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - PIO at 150MHz gives ~1500 cycles per bit - plenty of margin
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - Need debouncing (software or PIO delay)
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 4. Data Handling:
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - PIO can shift in 11-bit frames
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - Parity checking optional (often skipped)
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - Need FIFO for key/mouse codes
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - Multi-byte sequences for mouse movement
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 5. Bidirectional Considerations:
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - Host can send commands (LED control, reset)
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - Requires switching data pin direction
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat]    - May need separate TX/RX state machines
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] • Simplified Approach:*
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] Start with read-only implementation for keyboard, then add:
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] - Mouse support (3-byte packets)
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] - Command capability
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] - Error recovery (timeouts, resync)
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] • Integration:*
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] - Share PIO with video/audio or use separate PIO block
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] - Interrupt on data ready vs polling
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] - Queue for keycodes in main loop
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] The PIO can reliably handle the timing-critical bit sampling, leaving protocol decoding to the CPU.
2026-03-30 19:26:39 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:28:01 [simon@midnightblue] would shmitt trigger help for gpio
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] Yes, Schmitt triggers would help for PS/2 GPIO inputs. Here's why:
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] • Benefits:*
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] - Noise immunity: PS/2 uses open-collector with pull-ups, so signals can have slow edges.
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] Schmitt triggers provide hysteresis, cleaning up marginal transitions.
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] - Debouncing: Helps with contact bounce from mechanical switches (keyboards).
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] - Signal integrity: Important with longer cables or noisy environments.
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] • Pico GPIO Capabilities:*
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] - RP2040 GPIOs have programmable Schmitt triggers (enabled by default on most pins)
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] - Controlled via `padsbank0` registers
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] - Can be enabled/disabled per pin
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] • Implementation:*
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] // Enable Schmitt trigger on a pin
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] gpio_set_input_hysteresis_enabled(pin, true);
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] • Considerations:*
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] 1. Default state: Schmitt triggers are usually enabled on RP2040, but verify for your
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] specific pins
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] 2. Power consumption: Slightly higher with hysteresis enabled
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] 3. Speed trade-off: Schmitt triggers add small propagation delay (~ns), negligible for PS/2
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] speeds
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] 4. PS/2 specific: The clock line especially benefits since devices generate the clock
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] • Alternative/Complementary:*
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] - Software debouncing still needed for key bounce
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] - PIO can add sample delays to avoid glitches
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] - External RC filter if signal particularly noisy
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] For PS/2, enabling Schmitt triggers is recommended practice. The RP2040's built-in hysteresis
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] should be sufficient for most applications.
2026-03-30 19:28:14 [deepseek-chat] 
