2026-03-30 19:31:05 [simon@midnightblue] does the ps/2 protocol have a correct name?
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] Yes, the PS/2 protocol is properly called the "PS/2 interface" or "PS/2 connector
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] protocol".
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] • Technical Standards:*
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] - Originally defined by IBM in 1987 for the IBM Personal System/2 computers
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] - Standardized in IBM Technical Reference manuals
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] - Sometimes referred to as "AT keyboard interface" (the predecessor)
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] - The physical connector is officially "6-pin mini-DIN"
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] • Protocol Details:*
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] - Bidirectional serial protocol
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] - Clock typically driven by the device (keyboard/mouse)
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] - Often called "PS/2 communication protocol"
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] - Not to be confused with the PS/2 mouse port which uses the same connector but different data
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] format
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] • In Documentation:*
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] You'll typically see:
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] - "PS/2 keyboard interface"
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] - "PS/2 mouse protocol"
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] - "PS/2 device communication"
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] While "PS/2 protocol" is commonly understood, the more precise term is "PS/2 interface
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] protocol" when referring to the electrical and data communication specification.
2026-03-30 19:31:15 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:34:32 [simon@midnightblue] i have wired it up, gp0 is data and gp1 is clock, i like the idea of dedicated statemachine no interrupts
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] Good choice. Dedicated PIO state machine for PS/2 is clean. With your wiring:
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] • GPIO Assignment:*
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - GP0: PS/2 Data
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - GP1: PS/2 Clock
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - Both need pull-ups enabled (internal ~50kΩ should work)
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] • PIO State Machine Design:*
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - Clock pin as input, triggering on falling edges
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - Data sampled on clock edges
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - Shift in 11-bit frames (start + 8 data + parity + stop)
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - Push complete bytes to FIFO when stop bit received
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] • Advantages:*
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - No CPU interrupts during bit reception
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - PIO handles precise timing (≈60-100µs per bit)
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - FIFO buffers keycodes for main loop polling
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - Leaves CPU free for video/audio tasks
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] • Implementation Outline:*
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] 1. PIO waits for clock low (start bit)
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] 2. Samples data bits on 8 clock cycles
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] 3. Ignores parity/stop bits or validates them
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] 4. Pushes data byte to FIFO
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] 5. Returns to idle, watching for next start bit
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] • Integration:*
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - Poll PIO FIFO in main loop alongside video frame updates
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - Simple queue for keycode processing
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] - Error recovery on timeout (missing stop bit)
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] This approach matches your existing video/audio architecture well.
2026-03-30 19:34:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:36:21 [simon@midnightblue] both pins are inputs?
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] For a PS/2 keyboard/mouse (device-to-host communication), both pins should be
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] inputs on the RP2350 side.
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] • Correct Configuration:*
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] - GP0 (Data): Input with pull-up
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] - GP1 (Clock): Input with pull-up
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] • Why:*
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 1. PS/2 protocol: The keyboard/mouse generates both clock and data signals
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 2. Open-collector: Devices pull lines low, release to high (pull-ups provide high state)
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 3. Host role: Your RP2350 is the host - it listens, doesn't drive these lines during normal
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] operation
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] • Exception - Host to Device:*
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] If you need to send commands to the device (LED control, reset), you'd temporarily:
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 1. Pull Clock low for 100µs
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 2. Pull Data low (start bit)
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 3. Release Clock
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 4. Send bits with Clock/Data toggling
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 5. Return both to inputs when done
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] But for basic keycode reading, both pins remain inputs with pull-ups enabled.
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] • Setup:*
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] gpio_init(0); gpio_init(1);
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] gpio_set_dir(0, GPIO_IN); gpio_set_dir(1, GPIO_IN);
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] gpio_pull_up(0); gpio_pull_up(1);
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] This matches your "dedicated state machine no interrupts" approach - PIO monitors these input pins.
2026-03-30 19:36:33 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:36:51 [simon@midnightblue] both need shmitt option?
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] Yes, both pins should have Schmitt triggers enabled for PS/2.
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] • Why both:*
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] 1. Clock line: Critical for clean edge detection - PS/2 devices generate the clock with
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] open-collector
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] 2. Data line: Also open-collector, benefits from hysteresis for noise immunity
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] 3. Signal quality: PS/2 signals can have slow rise times (pull-up dependent)
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] • RP2350 Setup:*
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] gpio_set_input_hysteresis_enabled(0, true);  // Data
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] gpio_set_input_hysteresis_enabled(1, true);  // Clock
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] • Default State:*
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] On RP2350/Pico, Schmitt triggers are usually enabled by default on most GPIOs, but explicitly
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] enabling ensures:
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] - Consistent behavior across all pins
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] - Clear documentation in code
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] - Portability if code reused
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] • Note:* Schmitt triggers consume slightly more power, but for just two pins this is
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] negligible.
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] Given PS/2's relatively low speed (10-16.7 kHz) and open-collector signaling, the hysteresis
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] provides valuable noise margin for reliable operation.
2026-03-30 19:37:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:34 [simon@midnightblue] if they are default then no, would prefer clean code
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] Agreed. If Schmitt triggers are default-enabled on RP2350 GPIOs (which they typically are),
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] omit the explicit enable calls for cleaner code.
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] • Clean approach:*
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] // Just basic input setup
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] gpio_init(0);
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] gpio_init(1);
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] gpio_set_dir(0, GPIO_IN);
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] gpio_set_dir(1, GPIO_IN);
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] gpio_pull_up(0);
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] gpio_pull_up(1);
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] • Benefits:*
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] 1. Less code - fewer lines, easier to read
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] 2. Default behavior - uses chip's intended configuration
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] 3. Maintainability - no redundant settings
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] 4. Portability - works if defaults differ between RP variants
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] • Only add if:*
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] - You encounter glitches/bounce issues
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] - Porting to a system where defaults differ
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] - Debugging signal integrity problems
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] Your "dedicated state machine" approach with clean pin setup is the right choice. The PIO will
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] handle timing precisely regardless.
2026-03-30 19:37:43 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:39:10 [simon@midnightblue] can we start with pio code
2026-03-30 19:39:10 [simon@midnightblue] ; ps2.pio (c)2026 Simon Armstrong; ps/2 mouse keyboard port io ; in pins - DATA (GP0) CLK (GP1).program ps2.wrap
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] Here's a starting point for PS/2 PIO code:
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] ; ps2.pio (c)2026 Simon Armstrong
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] ; PS/2 mouse keyboard port I/O
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] ; in pins - DATA (GP0) CLK (GP1)
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] .program ps2
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] ; Wait for start condition (clock high, data low)
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] wait_start:
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     wait 1 pin 1        ; wait for clock high
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     wait 0 pin 0        ; wait for data low
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     wait 0 pin 1        ; wait for clock low (start bit)
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] ; Shift in 8 data bits
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     set x, 7            ; 8 bits (7..0)
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] bit_loop:
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     in pins, 1          ; sample data bit
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     wait 1 pin 1        ; wait for clock high
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     wait 0 pin 1        ; wait for clock low
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     jmp x--, bit_loop
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] ; Shift in parity and stop bits (optional)
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     in pins, 1          ; parity bit
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     wait 1 pin 1
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     wait 0 pin 1
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     in pins, 1          ; stop bit
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     wait 1 pin 1
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] ; Push data byte to FIFO
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     push noblock        ; push 8-bit data to RX FIFO
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat]     jmp wait_start
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] .wrap
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] • Key features:*
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] - Waits for proper start condition
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] - Samples 8 data bits on clock falling edges
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] - Pushes byte to FIFO (ignores parity/stop for simplicity)
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] - Returns to wait for next transmission
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] • Notes:*
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 1. Assumes pull-ups enabled on both pins
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 2. No error checking (timeouts, parity, framing)
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 3. Simple polling in main loop: `piosmgetrxfifo()`
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 4. May need timing adjustments for your setup
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] This provides a basic working receiver. You can enhance with parity checking, error recovery, or
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] host-to-device commands later.
2026-03-30 19:39:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] The PS/2 PIO code looks clean and functional. A few observations:
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] • Good:*
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] - Proper start condition detection
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] - 8 data bits + parity + stop bit sampling
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] - Data byte pushed to FIFO
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] - Minimal, efficient implementation
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] • Potential Considerations:*
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 1. No timeout/recovery: If a transmission gets interrupted (missing stop bit), the SM will
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] wait forever. Could add a timeout using PIO's timer or side-set.
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 2. Parity handling: You're sampling parity but not checking it. For reliability, you might
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] want to validate parity and discard bad frames.
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 3. FIFO overflow: If main loop doesn't read FIFO fast enough, `push noblock` will stall the
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] SM. Consider checking FIFO space or using interrupt.
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 4. Initial state: Should ensure pins are inputs with pull-ups before starting SM.
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] • Integration with main.cpp:*
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] You'll need to:
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 1. Initialize PS/2 pins as inputs with pull-ups
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 2. Load and start the PS/2 PIO program
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 3. Poll the RX FIFO in your main loop
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] • Simple addition to main():*
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] // PS/2 setup
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] gpio_init(0); gpio_init(1);
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] gpio_set_dir(0, GPIO_IN); gpio_set_dir(1, GPIO_IN);
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] gpio_pull_up(0); gpio_pull_up(1);
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] uint ps2_offset = pio_add_program(pio0, &ps2_program);
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] // Configure and start PS/2 SM (using SM 2 for example)
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] The code is a solid foundation. Test with a keyboard and see if you get scancodes in the FIFO.
2026-03-30 19:47:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] Good progress! A few issues to fix:
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] • Problems:*
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 1. PIO instance mismatch: You're using `pio2` but RP2350 has `pio0` and `pio1` blocks. PS/2
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] should use `pio0` or `pio1`.
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 2. Missing pin directions: PS/2 pins need to be set as inputs for the PIO.
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 3. Missing clock pin config: Only data pin configured in
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] `smconfigsetinpins`.
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] • Fix `ps2_init()`:*
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] void ps2_init(PIO pio, int sm) {
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat]     gpio_init(PS2_DATA);
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat]     gpio_set_dir(PS2_DATA, GPIO_IN);
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat]     gpio_pull_up(PS2_DATA);
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat]     gpio_init(PS2_CLK);
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat]     gpio_set_dir(PS2_CLK, GPIO_IN);
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat]     gpio_pull_up(PS2_CLK);
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat]     uint offset = pio_add_program(pio, &ps2_program);
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat]     pio_sm_config c = ps2_program_get_default_config(offset);
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat]     // Configure both pins for input
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat]     sm_config_set_in_pins(&c, PS2_DATA);
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat]     sm_config_set_jmp_pin(&c, PS2_CLK);  // For wait instructions
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat]     pio_sm_init(pio, sm, offset, &c);
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat]     pio_sm_set_enabled(pio, sm, true);
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] }
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] • In `main()`:*
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] // Change this line:
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] ps2_init(pio2,KeyboardStateMachine);
2026-03-30 19:57:05 [deepseek-chat] // To (use pio0 or pio1):
