2026-03-30 19:57:38 [simon@midnightblue] gpio default to inputs?
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] Yes, GPIOs default to inputs on RP2350/RP2040 after `gpio_init()`.
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] • From the datasheet:*
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] - After reset, all GPIOs are inputs
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] - `gpio_init(pin)` resets the pin to default state (input, no pulls, no functions)
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] - You don't need `gpiosetdir(pin, GPIO_IN)` explicitly
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] • So your PS/2 init can be simpler:*
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] void ps2_init(PIO pio, int sm) {
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat]     gpio_init(PS2_DATA);
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat]     gpio_pull_up(PS2_DATA);  // Input by default
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat]     gpio_init(PS2_CLK);
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat]     gpio_pull_up(PS2_CLK);   // Input by default
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat]     uint offset = pio_add_program(pio, &ps2_program);
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat]     pio_sm_config c = ps2_program_get_default_config(offset);
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat]     sm_config_set_in_pins(&c, PS2_DATA);
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat]     sm_config_set_jmp_pin(&c, PS2_CLK);
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat]     pio_sm_init(pio, sm, offset, &c);
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat]     pio_sm_set_enabled(pio, sm, true);
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] }
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] • Cleaner and correct.* The PIO will monitor these input pins without needing explicit
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] direction setting.
2026-03-30 19:57:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-30 20:00:34 [simon@midnightblue] hi, please check over the new code for ps2 keyboard support
2026-03-30 20:01:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] The new PS/2 keyboard support code looks well-structured. The PIO state machine correctly handles
2026-03-30 20:01:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] the PS/2 protocol by capturing data just after the falling edge of the clock.
2026-03-30 20:01:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:01:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] A few minor observations for your consideration:
2026-03-30 20:01:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:01:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Data Masking: Your PIO program shifts 10 bits total into the ISR (8 data bits, 1 parity
2026-03-30 20:01:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] bit, and 1 stop bit). Because the default shift direction is to the right, you will likely need to
2026-03-30 20:01:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] mask the resulting `scancode` variable in your C++ loop (e.g., bitwise AND with `0xFF`) to isolate
2026-03-30 20:01:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] the actual data byte.
2026-03-30 20:01:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Contiguous Pins: The `wait pol pin index` instructions in your PIO rely on the `IN` pin
2026-03-30 20:01:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] mapping base. This makes your code nicely relocatable, provided `PS2DATA` and `PS2CLK`
2026-03-30 20:01:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] remain contiguous GPIO pins in hardware (which they currently are at 0 and 1).
2026-03-30 20:01:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Unused JMP Pin: `smconfigsetjmppin` is configured in `ps2_init`, but the
2026-03-30 20:01:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] `jmp pin` instruction is not used in the assembly. This is harmless but can be safely removed to
2026-03-30 20:01:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] tidy up the initialization.
