2026-03-30 20:02:49 [simon@midnightblue] so will fifo be not empty with the 10 bits in it?
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, the FIFO will successfully receive a 32-bit word containing your 10 bits every time `push
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] noblock` executes.
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, there are two important details to adjust in your PIO code regarding bit alignment
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] and PS/2 clock timing:
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Bit Alignment: By default, the Input Shift Register (ISR) shifts right. Your 10 bits
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] will enter at the MSB (bit 31) and shift down. After 10 reads, your data will be left-aligned at
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] the top of the 32-bit word (bits 22–31), not at the bottom.
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Clock Timing: In your current loop, `in pins, 1` happens immediately after the start
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] bit's clock falls. This means it will read the start bit (0) as your first data bit. The
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] device changes data when the clock goes high, and the host should read when the clock goes low. You
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] need to wait for the clock cycle before reading the pin.
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the corrected `ps2.pio` that fixes the timing and adds an `in null, 22` to push your 10
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] bits down to the bottom of the 32-bit word:
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; ps2.pio (c)2026 Simon Armstrong
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; ps/2 mouse keyboard port io
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; in pins - DATA (GP0) CLK (GP1)
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] .program ps2
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; wait clock high, data low, clock low (Start bit)
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait	1 pin 1
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait	0 pin 0
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait	0 pin 1
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; data byte
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	x, 7
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] loop:
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait	1 pin 1	; wait clock high (device changes data)
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait	0 pin 1	; wait clock low (data is valid)
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	in	pins, 1	; read data bit
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	x--,loop
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; parity bit
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait	1 pin 1
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait	0 pin 1
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	in	pins, 1
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; stop bit
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait	1 pin 1
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait	0 pin 1
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	in	pins, 1
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; shift the 10 bits down to the LSBs (bits 0-9)
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	in	null, 22
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; push data to FIFO
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	push	noblock
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] .wrap
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] With this adjustment, your C++ code can easily extract the scancode byte by simply masking the
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] result:
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		if (!pio_sm_is_rx_fifo_empty(pio2, KeyboardStateMachine)) {
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			uint32_t raw_data = pio_sm_get(pio2, KeyboardStateMachine);
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			uint8_t scancode = raw_data & 0xFF; // Extracts the 8 data bits
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			// Process scancode here
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:03:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (Note: It is great to see the RP2350's `pio2` block being put to use here!)*
