2026-03-30 20:07:52 [simon@midnightblue] so default gpio is input with shmitt trigger?
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a review of your questions regarding the new PS/2 code:
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Will the FIFO be not empty with the 10 bits in it?*
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes. The `push noblock` instruction takes whatever is currently in the Input Shift Register (ISR)
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] and pushes it into the RX FIFO as a single 32-bit word, regardless of how many bits were shifted
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] in. Once pushed, `piosmisrxfifo_empty()` will return false, and your C++ code will
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] read that 32-bit word.
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Note on bit alignment:* Because you have not explicitly configured the `inshift` direction in
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] `ps2init()`, it defaults to shifting right. This means your 10 bits will be populated in the
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] highest bits of the 32-bit word (bits 22-31). If you prefer them at the bottom (bits 0-9), you will
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] need to configure the state machine to shift left.
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. So default GPIO is input with Schmitt trigger?*
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes. On the RP2040/RP2350 architectures, when you call `gpioinit()`, the pin defaults to an
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] input. Additionally, the hardware pad control registers (`PADSBANK0`) have the Schmitt trigger
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] enabled by default out of reset for all standard GPIOs, which is ideal for reading the somewhat
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] slow rise/fall times of a PS/2 clock line.
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Additional Observation on `ps2.pio`:*
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your `wait` instructions (e.g., `wait 1 pin 1`) use the `in` pin mapping index. Since you set the
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] `in` base pin to `PS2_DATA` (GPIO 0), `pin 0` maps to GPIO 0 (Data) and `pin 1` maps to GPIO 1
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] (Clock). This aligns perfectly with your wiring intent.
2026-03-30 20:08:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] The new PS/2 implementation looks solid. The PIO state machine correctly handles the PS/2 protocol
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] by sampling on the falling edge of the clock.
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] To answer your questions:
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Will the FIFO be not empty with the 10 bits in it?
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes. The `push noblock` instruction takes the entire 32-bit Input Shift Register (ISR) and pushes
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] it into the RX FIFO as a single 32-bit word. Because you read 10 bits and then explicitly shift in
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 22 zeros (`in null, 22`), your 10 bits are perfectly aligned at the bottom of that 32-bit word.
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] Once pushed, the RX FIFO contains one item, so `piosmisrxfifo_empty()` will return
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] `false` and your C++ loop will pick it up.
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Is the default GPIO an input with a Schmitt trigger?
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes. Calling `gpio_init()` sets the GPIO direction to input by default. Furthermore, the
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] RP2040/RP2350 hardware pad control registers have the Schmitt trigger enabled by default out of
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] reset, which is ideal for cleaning up the relatively slow edges of PS/2 clock and data lines.
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • One minor note on relocatability:*
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your PIO code, `wait 1 pin 1` looks at an offset of 1 from your configured `IN` base pin.
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because `PS2DATA` is 0 and `PS2CLK` is 1, this works perfectly. Just keep in mind that if
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] you ever remap the pins, the clock pin must always be strictly `PS2_DATA + 1` for the PIO `pin 1`
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] offset to target the clock line correctly.
2026-03-30 20:09:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:10:19 [simon@midnightblue] will mouse send through bytes similar to keyboard?
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] The updated code looks very solid. To answer your specific questions:
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Will the FIFO be not empty with the 10 bits in it?
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes. Your explicit `push noblock` instruction guarantees that the Input Shift Register (ISR) is
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] pushed into the RX FIFO. By adding `in null, 22` just before the push, you are perfectly padding
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] the 10 captured bits (8 data + 1 parity + 1 stop) into a complete 32-bit word. The C++ code will
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] see this as a non-empty FIFO and read the 32-bit value.
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Is the default GPIO an input with a Schmitt trigger?
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes. On the RP2040/RP2350 architectures, standard GPIO pads default to being inputs with the
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Schmitt trigger enabled. This hardware debouncing is highly beneficial for reading the relatively
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] slow and potentially noisy rising/falling edges of a PS/2 clock line.
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Will a mouse send through bytes similar to a keyboard?
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, absolutely. The physical layer protocol—1 start bit, 8 data bits (LSB first), 1 parity bit,
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] and 1 stop bit, all sampled on the falling edge of the clock—is completely identical for both PS/2
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] keyboards and mice. Your PIO state machine will capture mouse bytes exactly the same way it
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] captures keyboard bytes. The only difference is at the software level: a mouse typically groups
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] these bytes into 3-byte or 4-byte packets to report X/Y movement and button states, whereas a
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] keyboard sends distinct make/break scancodes.
2026-03-30 20:10:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
