2026-03-30 20:31:20 [simon@midnightblue] new mouse support compiles, but no data ever arrives
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your PIO logic for receiving PS/2 frames is spot on! The alignment with `in null, 22` and the clock
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] edge handling are perfectly implemented.
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] The reason no data arrives is due to the PS/2 mouse protocol itself. When a PS/2 mouse powers up,
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] it performs a self-test and sends `0xAA` `0x00`, but data reporting is disabled by default.
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] It will not send any movement packets until the host explicitly sends the "Enable Data Reporting"
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] command (`0xF4`).
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since your PIO program is receive-only, you'll need to bit-bang the `0xF4` command to the mouse
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] before initializing the PIO state machine.
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a reusable function to send a command to a PS/2 device:
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] void ps2_send_command(uint data_pin, uint clk_pin, uint8_t cmd) {
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     gpio_init(data_pin);
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     gpio_init(clk_pin);
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     gpio_pull_up(data_pin);
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     gpio_pull_up(clk_pin);
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // 1. Host pulls clock low for >100us to inhibit communication
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     gpio_set_dir(clk_pin, GPIO_OUT);
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     gpio_put(clk_pin, 0);
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     sleep_us(150);
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // 2. Host brings data low (Start bit) and releases clock
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     gpio_set_dir(data_pin, GPIO_OUT);
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     gpio_put(data_pin, 0);
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     gpio_set_dir(clk_pin, GPIO_IN);
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // 3. Wait for device to pull clock low
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     while (gpio_get(clk_pin)) tight_loop_contents();
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // 4. Send 8 data bits (LSB first)
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     bool parity = true;
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]         bool bit = (cmd >> i) & 1;
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]         gpio_put(data_pin, bit);
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]         parity ^= bit;
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]         while (!gpio_get(clk_pin)) tight_loop_contents();
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]         while (gpio_get(clk_pin)) tight_loop_contents();
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     }
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // 5. Send parity bit
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     gpio_put(data_pin, parity);
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     while (!gpio_get(clk_pin)) tight_loop_contents();
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     while (gpio_get(clk_pin)) tight_loop_contents();
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // 6. Release data, wait for device ACK and stop bit
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     gpio_set_dir(data_pin, GPIO_IN);
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     while (gpio_get(data_pin)) tight_loop_contents(); // Wait for ACK
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     while (!gpio_get(clk_pin)) tight_loop_contents();
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     while (gpio_get(clk_pin)) tight_loop_contents();
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] You can call this in `main()` right before you start the PIO:
2026-03-30 20:33:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
