2026-03-30 20:36:42 [simon@midnightblue] should a keyboard work without any fuss?
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] Hello!
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] To answer your question: Yes, a standard PS/2 keyboard should work without any fuss. Keyboards
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] automatically send a self-test passed byte (`0xAA`) on power-up and will immediately transmit
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] scancodes whenever a key is pressed.
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] A PS/2 mouse, however, will not send data automatically. While it also sends the initial `0xAA`
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] byte on power-up, it requires the host to explicitly send an "Enable Data Reporting" command
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] (`0xF4`) before it will begin transmitting movement and button packets.
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your current PIO implementation only handles device-to-host (RX) communication, the mouse
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] remains idle because it never receives the command to start sending data. To support a mouse, you
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] will need to implement host-to-device (TX) communication to send that initialization command.
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] As a quick hardware note: PS/2 devices generally require a 5V power supply to operate. Since the
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] RP2350 is a 3.3V device, ensure the devices are powered by 5V but that the data and clock lines are
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] operating as open-collector and are only being pulled up to 3.3V to protect your GPIO pins.
2026-03-30 20:37:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:38:15 [simon@midnightblue] power to ps2 connector is from vsys
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] A PS/2 keyboard should generally work without any fuss using a receive-only setup, as it
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] automatically sends scancodes whenever a key is pressed.
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] A PS/2 mouse, however, will not send any movement data until it is explicitly initialized by the
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] host. You must send the "Enable Data Reporting" command (`0xF4`) to the mouse before it will start
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] transmitting. Because your current PIO program is receive-only, the mouse is likely sitting idle
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] after its initial power-on self-test.
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] Regarding your power setup: powering the PS/2 connector from VSYS (typically 5V via USB) is
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] correct, as PS/2 devices require 5V. Fortunately, unlike the RP2040, the RP2350's GPIOs are 5V
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] tolerant, so directly connecting the clock and data lines is safe.
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] If your keyboard is also failing to register, the state machine might be hanging on the start bit
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] detection (`wait 0 pin 0`). In the PS/2 protocol, the device generates the clock, and data is valid
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] on the falling edge. If line noise causes a slight timing mismatch, waiting for the data line to go
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] low before the clock goes low can cause the PIO to desync and stall.
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know if you would like an example of how to adjust the PIO timing or handle bidirectional
2026-03-30 20:38:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] host-to-device communication.
