2026-03-30 20:55:13 [simon@midnightblue] how many pio can i have on rp2350?
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the answers to your questions regarding your RP2350 setup:
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Should a keyboard work without any fuss?*
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] A PS/2 keyboard generally works without fuss because it defaults to sending scan codes immediately
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] upon power-up and key presses. A PS/2 mouse, however, does not. A mouse requires the host to send
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] an initialization command (`0xF4` Enable Data Reporting) before it will transmit any movement or
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] button data. Because your current `ps2.pio` program is strictly configured for receiving (RX), it
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] cannot send the required activation command to the mouse.
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Powering the PS/2 connector from VSYS*
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] Using VSYS (typically ~5V if powered via USB) is correct for providing power, as standard PS/2
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] devices require 5V. However, please be cautious: RP2350 GPIO pins operate at 3.3V and are
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] not 5V tolerant. PS/2 devices pull the Clock and Data lines high to their supply voltage.
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] If they pull those lines up to 5V, it can damage the RP2350. You should use logic level shifters on
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] the Clock and Data lines to safely bridge the 5V device to the 3.3V microcontroller.
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. How many PIO can I have on RP2350?*
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] The RP2350 contains 3 independent PIO blocks (PIO0, PIO1, and PIO2). Each block contains 4
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] state machines, giving you a total of 12 state machines to work with. Your current code
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] correctly initializes all three blocks.
2026-03-30 20:55:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:56:39 [simon@midnightblue] what is difference between statemachine and block?
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the answers to your questions regarding the RP2350 and your PS/2 implementation:
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Why no mouse data arrives / Keyboard vs. Mouse fuss*
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] A PS/2 keyboard generally transmits scan codes immediately upon key presses, so a receive-only
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] implementation can work without much fuss. However, a PS/2 mouse will not send any movement data
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] until the host sends an initialization command (specifically `0xF4` to enable data reporting).
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your current PIO program is receive-only, the mouse is waiting for an initialization it
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] never receives.
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Powering PS/2 from VSYS*
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] VSYS provides roughly 5V (when powered via USB), which PS/2 devices require. However, the RP2350
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] GPIO pins are 3.3V logic. PS/2 devices use open-collector outputs, so relying on the Pico's
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] internal 3.3V pull-ups can sometimes work. For safety and reliability, it is highly recommended to
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] use external pull-ups to 3.3V and inline current-limiting resistors (or dedicated level shifters)
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] to prevent 5V spikes from damaging the RP2350.
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How many PIO on RP2350*
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] The RP2350 contains 3 PIO blocks (PIO0, PIO1, and PIO2).
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] • State Machine vs. Block*
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PIO Block: The overarching hardware peripheral. Each block contains a shared instruction
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] memory (32 slots) and shared interrupt flags.
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] • State Machine: The independent execution engine within a block. There are 4 state
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] machines per block. Each has its own program counter, FIFOs, shift registers, and clock divider,
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] but they all run programs stored in their parent block's shared memory.
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know if you would like code examples for bidirectional PS/2 communication or initialization.
2026-03-30 20:57:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
