2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the answers to your questions regarding the RP2350 and your PS/2 implementation:
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Mouse vs. Keyboard Initialization: A keyboard will generally work without any fuss; it
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] sends a Basic Assurance Test (BAT) byte on power-up and then transmits scancodes on key presses. A
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] PS/2 mouse, however, powers up in a disabled state. It requires the host to send an initialization
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] command (specifically `0xF4`, Enable Data Reporting) before it will transmit any movement packets.
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your current PIO program is read-only, it cannot send this initialization command to the
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] mouse.
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Powering from VSYS: VSYS typically provides ~5V when powered via USB, which is the
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] correct voltage for PS/2 devices. However, the RP2350 GPIO pins are strictly 3.3V tolerant. If the
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] PS/2 device pulls the clock and data lines up to 5V, it risks damaging the RP2350. You should use
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] level shifters or an open-drain configuration with diodes to protect the microcontroller pins.
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] • RP2350 PIO Count: The RP2350 features 3 independent PIO blocks (PIO0, PIO1, and PIO2).
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] • State Machine vs. Block: A PIO block is the physical hardware peripheral. Each PIO block
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] contains 4 independent state machines. The state machines are the actual execution units that run
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] the code, and all 4 state machines within a block share that block's resources.
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Instruction Memory: Yes, your understanding is correct. Each PIO block has a shared
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] memory of 32 instruction slots. You can load multiple distinct programs into a single PIO block, as
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] long as the combined total of instructions across all those programs does not exceed 32.
2026-03-30 21:00:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
