2026-04-01 20:17:05 [simon@midnightblue] i would like to fix the underlying issue, not the gemini crash
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] The `SyntaxError: Unexpected token 'r', "readtime "... is not valid JSON` occurs because the
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] model is returning the function name `readtime` as plain text instead of a JSON-formatted
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] string representing a tool call.
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] The issue lies in the `relay` function's logic for handling tool calls. Specifically, when the
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] model decides to call a tool, it must return a structured `tool_calls` object. If the model returns
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] raw text or an improperly formatted string, `JSON.parse` (on line 1126) fails.
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] To fix this, you need to ensure the `prepareGeminiPrompt` function and the relay logic enforce a
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] strict format.
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. Robust JSON Parsing
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] Modify `prepareGeminiPrompt` in `slopfountain.ts` to be more defensive. Instead of assuming the
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] input is valid JSON, wrap it in a `try...catch` and log the raw content if it fails.
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Replace this block in prepareGeminiPrompt
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] if (item.tool_calls) {
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]     const parts = item.tool_calls.map(tc => {
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]         let args;
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]         try {
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]             args = JSON.parse(tc.function.arguments);
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]         } catch (e) {
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]             echo("[RELAY] ERROR: Failed to parse tool arguments:", tc.function.arguments);
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]             args = {}; // Fallback
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]         }
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]         return {
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]             functionCall: {
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 name: tc.function.name,
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 args: args
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]             }
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]         };
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]     });
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]     contents.push({ role: "model", parts });
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. Validate Gemini Output
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `relay` function, Gemini's `model.generateContent` response might include function calls
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] that aren't properly serialized. Before calling `processToolCalls`, inspect the `calls` object:
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Inside relay()
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] const calls = completion.choices[0].message.tool_calls;
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] if (calls) {
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]     for (const call of calls) {
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]         if (!call.function || !call.function.arguments) {
2026-04-01 20:17:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]             echo("[RELAY] WARNING: Received malformed tool call from model");
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite]             continue;
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite]         }
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite]         // ... proceed to process
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Prompt Engineering (The "Why")
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] The model is likely defaulting to a conversational tone because the `systeminstruction` or the
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] `tools` definition isn't being strictly followed. Ensure your `rohaTools` definition (which you
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] pass to the model) has a very clear `description` field. If the model is confused about
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] when to call `readtime`, it often tries to "explain" that it is calling it, resulting
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] in the invalid string `read_time ...` rather than the JSON object.
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Recommendation:* Update the `read_time` description to be more imperative:
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] {
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite]     type: "function",
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite]     function: {
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite]         name: "read_time",
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite]         description: "Always call this function when asked for the current time. Output only the JSON tool call format.",
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite]         // ...
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] By making the parser defensive and tightening the system prompt, you will stop the crash and force
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] the model to adhere to the required JSON protocol.
2026-04-01 20:17:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
