2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] This confirms the issue: one model is injecting non-JSON text into the `tool` message
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] history, which later causes the Gemini `relay` function to crash when it attempts to
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] `JSON.parse` that corrupted history entry.
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] To fix this, you must sanitize the `tool` role messages before they are processed by `relay`.
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. Identify the Source
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] The `tool` role messages in your `rohaHistory` are being polluted. Find where `rohaHistory.push`
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] occurs with `role: "tool"`. It is likely in `processToolCalls`:
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Current logic in processToolCalls
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] const item = { role: "tool", title, tool_call_id: id, content };
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] rohaHistory.push(item);
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] If `content` is being set to a raw string like `"read_time"` instead of a valid JSON string (e.g.,
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] `"{ \"time\": \"...\" }"`), the downstream `relay` function will crash.
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. Implement a Sanitizer/Validator
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] Add a validation layer in `processToolCalls` to ensure that if a model injects a non-JSON string,
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] it is either corrected or stripped before it enters the history.
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] Modify your `processToolCalls` function in `slopfountain.ts`:
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] async function processToolCalls(calls) {
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]     const results = [];
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]     for (const tool of calls) {
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]         // ... existing logic ...
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]         try {
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]             const result = await onCall(tool);
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]             // --- FIX: Ensure content is strictly valid JSON ---
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]             let jsonContent;
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]             try {
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 jsonContent = JSON.stringify(result || { success: false });
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]             } catch (e) {
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 // If it can't be stringified, fallback to a safe error JSON
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 jsonContent = JSON.stringify({ error: "Invalid result format" });
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]             }
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]             results.push({
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 tool_call_id: tool.id,
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 name: tool.function.name,
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 content: jsonContent // Ensure this is always a stringified JSON
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]             });
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]         } catch (e) {
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]             // ... handle error ...
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]         }
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]     return results;
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Defensive Parsing in `relay`
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] Even with the fix above, you should make the `relay` function's `plainHistory` (or the function
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] generating the prompt) resilient to bad data. Update `plainHistory` to skip or clean corrupted
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] entries:
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] case "tool":
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Ensure content is valid JSON before passing to the model
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]     let cleanContent = item.content;
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]     try {
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]         JSON.parse(cleanContent);
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]     } catch (e) {
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]         // If content is corrupted (e.g., "read_time"), replace with an empty JSON object
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]         echo("[RELAY] Warning: Corrupted tool history found, sanitizing...");
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]         cleanContent = "{}";
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]     list.push({ role: "tool", tool_call_id: item.tool_call_id, name: item.name, content: cleanContent });
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite]     break;
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Why this works:*
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Problem: A previous model (e.g., DeepSeek or Grok) is incorrectly formatting the
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] `tool` message content.
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Fix: You are now forcing the `tool` message content to be valid JSON before
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] it is pushed to the history (`processToolCalls`) and before it is sent to the next model
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] (`relay`). This prevents the `JSON.parse` at line 1126 from ever seeing the raw string
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] `"read_time"`.
2026-04-01 20:18:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:21:48 [simon@midnightblue] what method does deepseek and grok xai use to add tool results to history?
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] In the `slopfountain.ts` code you provided, both DeepSeek and xAI (via the `OpenAI` API
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] implementation) follow the standard OpenAI Chat Completions pattern for tool results.
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] The history integration happens in the `relay()` function. Here is the breakdown of the mechanism:
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The Tool Call Cycle
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] When the model returns a `tool_calls` object in its response, the relay captures it:
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] const calls = choice.message.tool_calls;
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] // ... validation logic ...
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] const toolCalls = calls.map((tool, index) => ({
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     id: tool.id,
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     type: "function",
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     function: {name: tool.function.name, arguments: tool.function.arguments || "{}"}
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] }));
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] // The assistant's request to use the tool is pushed to history
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] rohaHistory.push({role:"assistant", name:payload.model, mut, content:choice.message.content, tool_calls:toolCalls});
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. Executing the Tool
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] The code then calls `processToolCalls(calls)`, which executes your local functions (`readtime`,
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] `submitfile`, etc.) and returns an array of results.
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Adding Results to History (The "tool" role)
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is where the history is updated to satisfy the model's expectation of a tool response:
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] const toolResults = await processToolCalls(calls);
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] for (const result of toolResults) {
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     const id = result.tool_call_id;
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     const title = "ToolCall:" + id;
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     const content = result.name + " replied " + result.content;
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     const item = { role: "tool", title, tool_call_id: id, content };
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // This pushes the result into the rohaHistory array
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     rohaHistory.push(item);
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why this is likely corrupting your history:
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] Looking at your `onCall` function and the `processToolCalls` implementation:
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Format Mismatch: In `processToolCalls`, you are setting `content` to:
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     `result.name + " replied " + result.content`
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   If `result.content` is an object (which `onCall` returns, e.g., `{time: "..."}`), it is
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] being stringified via `JSON.stringify(result)`.
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   The Bug: If `onCall` ever returns a raw string or an improperly formatted object,
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] `JSON.stringify` might produce something that isn't a valid JSON representation of a tool result,
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] or it might just be the string itself.
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  The "read_time" Corruption:
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     Your `read_time` case in `onCall` returns: `return {time, tz, locale};`.
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     If another part of your code or a different model is manually pushing to `rohaHistory` with
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] `role: "tool"` but providing a plain string instead of the `{role: "tool",
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] toolcallid: "...", content: "..."}` object structure, the `plainHistory` function will fail
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] to serialize it correctly for the next API call.
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  The Gemini Crash:
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     When you switch to Gemini, your `prepareGeminiPrompt` function does this:
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     case "tool":{
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]         const functionResponse = {name: item.name, response: JSON.parse(text)};
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]         contents.push({role:"function", parts:[{functionResponse}] });
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     If `item.content` (which is `text`) is not a valid JSON string (because a previous model pushed
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] a raw string like `"read_time"`), `JSON.parse(text)` throws the SyntaxError you are seeing.
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] Summary
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] The OpenAI-compatible models (DeepSeek, xAI) are not the ones crashing the system; they are
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] the ones writing the potentially malformed tool history. Gemini is simply the "strict"
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] consumer that crashes when it tries to read that corrupted history. You need to ensure that every
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] `role: "tool"` entry in `rohaHistory` has a `content` field that is a strictly valid JSON
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] string.
2026-04-01 20:22:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:27 [simon@midnightblue] i see a bunch of
2026-04-01 20:25:27 [simon@midnightblue] 				list.push({...item});
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] That line `list.push({...item});` inside the `plainHistory`, `strictHistory`, `multiHistory`, and
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] `inlineHistory` functions is the primary culprit.
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] By using the spread operator `{...item}`, you are copying the object exactly as it exists in
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] `rohaHistory`. If an earlier model or a manual tool-call injection pushed an item where `content`
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] is a raw string (like `"read_time"`) instead of a JSON string, that "dirty" object is being passed
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] directly into the prompt payload for the next model.
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Fix: Force Serialization
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] You need to ensure that when any history-processing function encounters a `tool` role item, the
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] `content` is explicitly a stringified JSON object.
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] Update your history-flattening functions (specifically `plainHistory`, `strictHistory`, etc.) to
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] sanitize the `tool` role content:
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Inside plainHistory (and apply this pattern to the others)
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] case "tool":
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Instead of: list.push({...item});
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Use this:
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]     list.push({
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]         role: "tool",
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]         tool_call_id: item.tool_call_id,
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]         name: item.name,
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]         // Ensure content is a valid JSON string
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]         content: typeof item.content === 'string'
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]             ? (isJson(item.content) ? item.content : JSON.stringify({ raw_output: item.content }))
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]             : JSON.stringify(item.content)
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]     });
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]     break;
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] You will need a helper function like this:
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] function isJson(str: string) {
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]     try {
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]         JSON.parse(str);
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]         return true;
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]     } catch (e) {
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]         return false;
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why this is happening:
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  The "Dirty" Push: Somewhere in your code, an item is being pushed to `rohaHistory` with
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] `role: "tool"` where the `content` is just a plain string (e.g., `"read_time"`).
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  The Spread Trap: The `{...item}` spread operator preserves that bad string.
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  The Gemini Crash: When `prepareGeminiPrompt` is called for Gemini, it reaches:
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]     case "tool":{
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]         const functionResponse={name:item.name,response:JSON.parse(text)}; // <-- CRASH
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite]     `text` is the `content` from the history item. If `text` is `"readtime"`,
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] `JSON.parse("readtime")` throws the `SyntaxError` you are seeing.
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Immediate Action:*
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] Search your codebase for `rohaHistory.push` and find where `role: "tool"` is being set. Ensure the
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] `content` field being passed there is always the result of `JSON.stringify(...)`.
2026-04-01 20:25:32 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
